@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2129 = private unnamed_addr constant [19 x i8] c"nonlocal-and-global", align 1
@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2130 = private unnamed_addr constant [872 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for variables which are both declared as both `nonlocal` and\0A`global`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AA `nonlocal` variable is a variable that is defined in the nearest\0Aenclosing scope, but not in the global scope, while a `global` variable is\0Aa variable that is defined in the global scope.\0A\0ADeclaring a variable as both `nonlocal` and `global` is contradictory and\0Awill raise a `SyntaxError`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Acounter = 0\0A\0A\0Adef increment():\0A    global counter\0A    nonlocal counter\0A    counter += 1\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Acounter = 0\0A\0A\0Adef increment():\0A    global counter\0A    counter += 1\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: The `global` statement](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/simple_stmts.html#the-global-statement)\0A- [Python documentation: The `nonlocal` statement](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/simple_stmts.html#nonlocal)\0A", align 1
