@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2201 = private unnamed_addr constant [14 x i8] c"sorted-min-max", align 1
@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2202 = private unnamed_addr constant [1174 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `sorted()` to retrieve the minimum or maximum value in\0Aa sequence.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AUsing `sorted()` to compute the minimum or maximum value in a sequence is\0Ainefficient and less readable than using `min()` or `max()` directly.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Anums = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5]\0Alowest = sorted(nums)[0]\0Ahighest = sorted(nums)[-1]\0Ahighest = sorted(nums, reverse=True)[0]\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Anums = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5]\0Alowest = min(nums)\0Ahighest = max(nums)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AIn some cases, migrating to `min` or `max` can lead to a change in behavior,\0Anotably when breaking ties.\0A\0AAs an example, `sorted(data, key=itemgetter(0), reverse=True)[0]` will return\0Athe _last_ \22minimum\22 element in the list, if there are multiple elements with\0Athe same key. However, `min(data, key=itemgetter(0))` will return the _first_\0A\22minimum\22 element in the list in the same scenario.\0A\0AAs such, this rule's fix is marked as unsafe when the `reverse` keyword is used.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `min`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#min)\0A- [Python documentation: `max`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#max)\0A", align 1
